  "full_response": "Observability in software systems refers to the capability of the system to be understood based on its internal state, that is, the ability to deduce the internal processes and system behaviors from external outputs and events. Observability is made possible by instrumenting the system with logging, metrics, and distributed tracing. With adequate observability, you can answer three questions about your system: What happened? Why did it happen? When did it happen?\n\nOn the other hand, monitoring in software systems mainly concentrates on measuring and recording the operational information of a system such as CPU, memory usage, disk I/O, and network traffic. The main goal monitoring is to quickly detect and alert anomalies and incidents that might affect the service's performance or availability.\n\nWhile observability promotes a complete view and understanding of the system, monitoring focuses only on the key indicators. Both are crucial practices for maintaining reliable and healthy software systems to guarantee that services are running correctly, thereby detecting and addressing incidents in a timely manner.\n\nIn conclusion, while observability and monitoring are closely related, monitoring is a subset of observability. Every monitoring set up counts as an observable system, but not every observable system necessarily includes the measuring and recording aspects of monitoring. This means you can have a theoretically observable system without implementing monitoring on it.",
